# DTF tier manifest: promotion-chain-and-peer-push  (artifact-keeper#6546, #6549; 1.13.0)
# Profile-set: storage.s3 (MinIO). A cloud backend is what shares one
# content-addressed key between every repository holding an image.
#
# THE BUGS
#   #6546 the promotion write guard exempted only the source and the target of
#         a promotion. After dev -> stage, dev still owns the image's
#         oci-manifests/<digest> key, so stage -> prod was 409 "already owned
#         by another repository". Fixed: a repository holding the same bytes
#         under that key does not block the hop.
#   #6549 a promoted copy fired artifact.uploaded but queued no push sync task,
#         so a peer subscribed to the target repository never received it.
#         Fixed: single, bulk and approval promotions queue the push like an
#         upload.
#
# THE PEER. As in npm-unpublish-and-push-sync: registered at an unresolvable
# `.invalid` host, then set online with a day of backoff in the DB so the sync
# worker never claims a row. sync_tasks is the observable.
#
# THE GATES
#   (CONTROL) both images: an image pushed to dev (docker staging) promotes to
#             stage (docker local) with 200; a plain upload to a
#             push-subscribed generic repository queues a push row.
#   (FIX)     #6546: the same manifest promotes from stage to prod with 200 and
#             prod serves it (red on main: 409).
#             #6549: a single promotion and a bulk promotion into the
#             push-subscribed generic repository each queue a push row for the
#             copy, and the docker promotion into the subscribed stage does
#             too (red on main: no row).
PROFILES="storage.s3"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
COVERS="6546 6549"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
